Instagram isn't just for doomscrolling. It's actually a masterclass in UX.

Lately, my social feed has taught me more about practical product design than most textbooks. I’ve been quietly consuming content on UX fundamentals, layout optimizations, and perceived performance.

One concept completely blew my mind: How to make loading screens feel faster than they actually are.

It’s called Optimistic UI.

Instead of forcing users to stare at a boring loading spinner, the frontend instantly updates to show the successful state before the server even responds.

Think about it:
👉 When you click "Add to Cart" on Amazon or Flipkart, the counter ticks up immediately.
👉 When you double-tap a post on Instagram, the heart lights up instantly.

The backend catches up a split second later, but to the user, the app feels lightning fast. It's all about psychological perception.

I spent the last two weeks obsessing over this concept and rewriting our frontend logic.
The result? Our websites now feel incredibly snappy, fluid, and instant. It completely transforms the user experience without actually changing our server speeds.

The biggest takeaway? Never underestimate unconventional learning sources. Sometimes your next engineering or design breakthrough is hiding right in your feed.

What’s the most valuable thing you’ve learned from a "non-educational" platform? Let me know below! 👇

